.TH "SEP" "1" "Oct 09, 2026" "1.4" "sep"
.SH NAME
sep \- sep Documentation
.sp
\fIPython library for Source Extraction and Photometry\fP
.SH ABOUT
.sp
Source Extractor \%<http://\:www\:.astromatic\:.net/\:software/\:sextractor>
(Bertin & Arnouts 1996) is a widely used
command\-line program for segmentation and analysis of astronomical
images. It reads in FITS format files, performs a configurable series
of tasks, including background estimation, source detection,
deblending and a wide array of source measurements, and finally
outputs a FITS format catalog file.
.sp
While Source Extractor is highly useful, the fact that it can only be
used as an executable can limit its applicability or lead to awkward
workflows. There is often a desire to have programmatic access to
perform one or more of the above tasks on in\-memory images as part of
a larger custom analysis.
.sp
\fBSEP makes the core algorithms of Source Extractor available as a
library of stand\-alone functions and classes.\fP These operate directly
on in\-memory arrays (no FITS files or configuration files).  The code
is derived from the Source Extractor code base (written in C) and aims
to produce results compatible with Source Extractor whenever possible.
SEP consists of a C library with no dependencies outside the standard
library, and a Python module that wraps the C library in a Pythonic
API. The Python wrapper operates on NumPy arrays with NumPy as its
only dependency. See below for language\-specfic build and usage
instructions.
.sp
\fBSome features:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
spatially variable background and noise estimation
.IP \(bu 2
source extraction, with on\-the\-fly convolution and source deblending
.IP \(bu 2
circular and elliptical aperture photometry
.IP \(bu 2
fast: implemented in C with Python bindings via Cython
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional features not in Source Extractor:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Optimized matched filter for variable noise in source extraction.
.IP \(bu 2
Circular annulus and elliptical annulus aperture photometry functions.
.IP \(bu 2
Local background subtraction in shape consistent with aperture in
aperture photometry functions.
.IP \(bu 2
Exact pixel overlap mode in all aperture photometry functions.
.IP \(bu 2
Masking of elliptical regions on images.
.UNINDENT
.SS SEP, SEP\-PJW, and Package Names
.sp
\fBsep\fP was originally released by Kyle Barbary, at
kbarbary/sep \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:kbarbary/\:sep> (\fBsep<=1.2.1\fP). For a
brief period, the package was maintained by Peter Watson, under the
\fBsep\-pjw\fP package name, at
PJ\-Watson/sep\-pjw \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:PJ-Watson/\:sep-pjw> and
PyPI/sep\-pjw \%<https://\:pypi\:.org/\:project/\:sep-pjw/>
(\fB1.3.0<=sep\-pjw<=1.3.8\fP). Both of these repositories will be archived,
and future development will take place at
sep\-developers/sep \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:sep-developers/\:sep>
(\fBsep>=1.4.0\fP).
Note that there may be some incompatibilities between \fBsep==1.2.1\fP and
\fBsep==1.4.0\fP when using the C\-API directly \-\- the changes are documented
here \%<>\&.
.SH INSTALLATION
.SS with conda
.sp
SEP can be installed with conda from the \fBconda\-forge\fP channel:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
conda install \-c conda\-forge sep
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS with pip
.sp
SEP can also be installed with pip \%<https://\:pip\:.pypa\:.io>\&. After
ensuring that numpy is installed, run
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
python \-m pip install sep
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If you get an error about permissions, you are probably using your
system Python. In this case, I recommend using pip\(aqs \(dquser install\(dq \%<https://\:pip\:.pypa\:.io/\:en/\:latest/\:user_guide/#\:user-installs> option to
install sep into your user directory
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
python \-m pip install \-\-user sep
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Do \fBnot\fP install \fBsep\fP or other third\-party Python packages using
\fBsudo\fP unless you are fully aware of the risks.
.SH USAGE GUIDE
.SS Matched Filter (Convolution)
.sp
For source detection, SEP supports using a matched filter, which can
give the optimal detection signal\-to\-noise for objects with some known
shape. This is controlled using the \fBfilter_kernel\fP keyword in
\fBsep.extract\fP \%<#\:sep\:.extract>\&. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
import sep

kernel = np.array([[1., 2., 3., 2., 1.],
                   [2., 3., 5., 3., 2.],
                   [3., 5., 8., 5., 3.],
                   [2., 3., 5., 3., 2.],
                   [1., 2., 3., 2., 1.]])
objects = sep.extract(data, thresh, filter_kernel=kernel)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If \fBfilter_kernel\fP is not specified, a default 3\-by\-3 kernel
is used. To disable filtering entirely, specify \fBfilter_kernel=None\fP\&.
.sp
What array should be used for \fBfilter_kernel\fP? It should be
approximately the shape of the objects you are trying to detect. For
example, to optimize for the detection of point sources,
\fBfilter_kernel\fP should be set to shape of the point spread function
(PSF) in the data. For galaxy detection, a larger kernel could be
used. In practice, anything that is roughly the shape of the desired
object works well since the main goal is to negate the effects of
background noise, and a reasonable estimate is good enough.
.SS Correct treatment in the presence of variable noise
.sp
In Source Extractor, the matched filter is implemented assuming there
is equal noise across all pixels in the kernel. The matched filter
then simplifies to a convolution of the data with the kernel. In
\fBsep.extract\fP \%<#\:sep\:.extract>, this is also the behavior when there is constant noise
(when \fBerr\fP is not specified).
.sp
In the presence of independent noise on each pixel, SEP uses a full
matched filter implementation that correctly accounts for the noise in
each pixel. This is not available in Source Extractor. Some benefits
of this method are that detector sensitivity can be taken into account
and edge effects are handled gracefully. For example, suppose we have
an image with noise that is higher in one region than another. This
can often occur when coadding images:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# create a small image with higher noise in the upper left
n = 16
X, Y = np.meshgrid(np.arange(n), np.arange(n))
mask = Y > X
error = np.ones((n, n))
error[mask] = 4.0
data = error * np.random.normal(size=(n, n))

# add source to middle of data
source = 3.0 * np.array([[1., 2., 1.],
                         [2., 4., 2.],
                         [1., 2., 1.]])
m = n // 2 \- 1
data[m:m+3, m:m+3] += source

plt.imshow(data, interpolation=\(aqnearest\(aq, origin=\(aqlower\(aq, cmap=\(aqbone\(aq)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
[image]
.sp
Specifying \fBfilter_type=\(aqconv\(aq\fP will use simple convolution, matching the
behavior of Source Extractor. The object is not detected:
.sp
.EX
>>> objects = sep.extract(data, 3.0, err=error, filter_type=\(aqconv\(aq)
>>> len(objects)
0
.EE
.sp
Setting \fBfilter_type=\(aqmatched\(aq\fP (the default)
correctly deweights the noisier pixels around the source and detects
the object:
.sp
.EX
>>> objects = sep.extract(data, 3.0, err=error, filter_type=\(aqmatched\(aq)
>>> len(objects)
1
.EE
.SS Derivation of the matched filter formula
.sp
Assume that we have an image containing a single point source. This
produces a signal with PSF S_i and noise N_i at each pixel
indexed by i\&. Then the measured image data D_i (i.e. our
pixel values) is given by:
.sp
.ce
D_i = S_i + N_i


.ce 0
.sp
Then we want to apply a linear transformation T_i which gives an
output Y:
.sp
.ce
Y = \esum_i T_i D_i = T^T D


.ce 0
.sp
We use matrix notation from here on and drop the explicit sums. Our
objective is to find the transformation T_i which maximizes the
signal\-to\-noise ratio SNR\&.
.sp
.ce
SNR^2 = \efrac{(T^T S)^2}{E[(T^T N)^2]}


.ce 0
.sp
We can expand the denominator as:
.sp
.ce
E[(T^T N)^2] = E[(T^T N)(N^T T)] = T^T \ecdot E[N N^T] \ecdot T
= T^T C T


.ce 0
.sp
Where C_{ik} is the covariance of the noise between pixels
i and k\&. Now using the Cauchy\-Schwarz inequality on the
numerator:
.sp
.ce
(T^T S)^2 = (T^T C^{1/2} C^{\-1/2} S)^2 \ele (T^T C^{1/2})^2
(C^{\-1/2} S)^2 = (T^T C T) (S^T C^{\-1} S)


.ce 0
.sp
since C^T = C\&. The signal\-to\-noise ratio is therefore bounded by:
.sp
.ce
&SNR^2 \ele \efrac{(T^T C T)(S^T C^{\-1} S)}{(T^T C T)} \e\e
&SNR^2 \ele S^T C^{\-1} S


.ce 0
.sp
Choosing T = \ealpha C^{\-1} S where \ealpha is an arbitrary
normalization constant, we get equality. Hence this choise of T is
the optimal linear tranformation. We normalize this linear transformation
so that if there is no signal and only noise, we get an expected
signal\-to\-noise ratio of 1. With this definition, the output SNR
represents the number of standard deviations above the background. This
gives:
.sp
.ce
&E[(T^T N)^2] = T^T C T = \ealpha^2 S^T C^{\-1} C C^{\-1} S = \ealpha^2
S^T C^{\-1} S = 1 \e\e
&\ealpha = \efrac{1}{\esqrt{S^T C^{\-1} S}}


.ce 0
.sp
Putting everything together, our normalized linear transformation is:
.sp
.ce
T = \efrac{C^{\-1} S}{\esqrt{S^T C^{\-1} S}}


.ce 0
.sp
And the optimal signal\-to\-noise is given in terms of the known variables
as:
.sp
.ce
SNR = \efrac{S^T C^{\-1} D}{\esqrt{S^T C^{\-1} S}}


.ce 0
.SS Aperture photometry
.sp
There are four aperture functions available:
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Function
T}	T{
Sums data within...
T}
_
T{
\fBsep.sum_circle\fP \%<#\:sep\:.sum_circle>
T}	T{
circle(s)
T}
_
T{
\fBsep.sum_circann\fP \%<#\:sep\:.sum_circann>
T}	T{
circular annulus/annuli
T}
_
T{
\fBsep.sum_ellipse\fP \%<#\:sep\:.sum_ellipse>
T}	T{
ellipse(s)
T}
_
T{
\fBsep.sum_ellipann\fP \%<#\:sep\:.sum_ellipann>
T}	T{
elliptical annulus/annuli
T}
.TE
.sp
The follow examples demonstrate options for circular aperture
photometry. The other functions behave similarly.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# sum flux in circles of radius=3.0
flux, fluxerr, flag = sep.sum_circle(data, objs[\(aqx\(aq], objs[\(aqy\(aq], 3.0)

# x, y and r can be arrays and obey numpy broadcasting rules.
# Here, r is an array instead of a single number:
flux, fluxerr, flag = sep.sum_circle(data, objs[\(aqx\(aq], objs[\(aqy\(aq],
                                     3.0 * np.ones(len(objs)))

# use a different subpixel sampling (default is 5; 0 means \(dqexact\(dq)
flux, fluxerr, flag = sep.sum_circle(data, objs[\(aqx\(aq], objs[\(aqy\(aq], 3.0,
                                     subpix=0)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBError calculation\fP
.sp
In the default modes illustrated above, the uncertainty \fBfluxerr\fP is
not calculated and values of 0 are simply returned. The uncertainty can be
flexibly and efficiently calculated, depending on the characteristics
of your data. The presence of an \fBerr\fP or \fBvar\fP keyword indicates
a per\-pixel noise, while the presense of a \fBgain\fP keyword indicates
that the Poisson uncertainty on the total sum should be included. Some
illustrative examples:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# Specify a per\-pixel \(dqbackground\(dq error and a gain. This is suitable
# when the data have been background subtracted.
flux, fluxerr, flag = sep.sum_circle(data, objs[\(aqx\(aq], objs[\(aqy\(aq], 3.0,
                                     err=bkg.globalrms, gain=1.0)

# Variance can be passed instead of error, with identical results.
flux, fluxerr, flag = sep.sum_circle(data, objs[\(aqx\(aq], objs[\(aqy\(aq], 3.0,
                                     var=bkg.globalrms**2, gain=1.0)

# Error or variance can be arrays, indicating that the background error
# is variable.
bkgrms = bkg.rms()  # array, same shape as data
flux, fluxerr, flag = sep.sum_circle(data, objs[\(aqx\(aq], objs[\(aqy\(aq], 3.0,
                                     err=bkgrms, gain=1.0)

# If your uncertainty array already includes Poisson noise from the object,
# leave gain as None (default):
flux, fluxerr, flag = sep.sum_circle(data, objs[\(aqx\(aq], objs[\(aqy\(aq], 3.0,
                                     err=error_array)

# If your data represent raw counts (it is not background\-subtracted),
# set only gain to get the poisson error:
flux, fluxerr, flag = sep.sum_circle(data, objs[\(aqx\(aq], objs[\(aqy\(aq], 3.0,
                                     gain=1.0)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The error is calculated as
.sp
.ce
\esigma_F^2 = \esum_i \esigma_i^2 + F/g
.ce 0
.sp
where the sum is over pixels in the aperture, \esigma_i is the
noise in each pixel, F is the sum in the aperture and
g is the gain. The last term is not added if \fBgain\fP is
\fINone\fP\&.
.sp
\fBMasking\fP
.sp
Apply a mask (same shape as data). Pixels where the mask is True are
\(dqcorrected\(dq to the average value within the aperture.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
flux, fluxerr, flag = sep.sum_circle(data, objs[\(aqx\(aq], objs[\(aqy\(aq], 3.0,
                                     mask=mask)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBLocal background subtraction\fP
.sp
The \fBsum_circle\fP \%<#\:sep\:.sum_circle> and \fBsum_ellipse\fP \%<#\:sep\:.sum_ellipse> functions have options
for performing local background subtraction. For example, to subtract the
background calculated in an annulus between 6 and 8 pixel radius:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
flux, fluxerr, flag = sep.sum_circle(data, objs[\(aqx\(aq], objs[\(aqy\(aq], 3.0,
                                     mask=mask, bkgann=(6., 8.))
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Pixels in the background annulus are not subsampled and any masked
pixels in the annulus are completely igored rather than corrected.
The inner and outer radii can also be arrays. The error in the background
is included in the reported error.
.SS Equivalent of FLUX_AUTO (e.g., MAG_AUTO) in Source Extractor
.sp
This is a two\-step process. First we calculate the Kron radius for each
object, then we perform elliptical aperture photometry within that radius:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
kronrad, krflag = sep.kron_radius(data, x, y, a, b, theta, 6.0)
flux, fluxerr, flag = sep.sum_ellipse(data, x, y, a, b, theta, 2.5*kronrad,
                                      subpix=1)
flag |= krflag  # combine flags into \(aqflag\(aq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This specific example is the equilvalent of setting \fBPHOT_AUTOPARAMS
2.5, 0.0\fP in Source Extractor (note the 2.5 in the argument to
\fBsep.sum_ellipse\fP). The second Source Extractor parameter is a
minimum diameter. To replicate Source Extractor behavior for non\-zero
values of the minimum diameter, one would put in logic to use circular
aperture photometry if the Kron radius is too small. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
r_min = 1.75  # minimum diameter = 3.5
use_circle = kronrad * np.sqrt(a * b) < r_min
cflux, cfluxerr, cflag = sep.sum_circle(data, x[use_circle], y[use_circle],
                                        r_min, subpix=1)
flux[use_circle] = cflux
fluxerr[use_circle] = cfluxerr
flag[use_circle] = cflag
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBWarning:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Caution should be used when calculating Kron radii in crowded fields.
In almost all cases, one would want to pass in a segmentation map to
mask out nearby  objects, as described below in
Segmentation\-masked image measurements\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Equivalent of FLUX_RADIUS in Source Extractor
.sp
In Source Extractor, the FLUX_RADIUS parameter gives the radius of a
circle enclosing a desired fraction of the total flux. For example,
with the setting \fBPHOT_FLUXFRAC 0.5\fP, FLUX_RADIUS will give the
radius of a circle containing half the \(dqtotal flux\(dq of the object. For
the definition of \(dqtotal flux\(dq, Source Extractor uses its measurement
of FLUX_AUTO, which is taken through an elliptical aperture (see
above). Thus, with the setting \fBPHOT_FLUXFRAC 1.0\fP, you would find
the circle containing the same flux as whatever ellipse Source
Extractor used for \fBFLUX_AUTO\fP\&.
.sp
Given a previous calculation of \fBflux\fP as above, calculate the
radius for a flux fraction of 0.5:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
r, flag = sep.flux_radius(data, objs[\(aqx\(aq], objs[\(aqy\(aq], 6.*objs[\(aqa\(aq], 0.5,
                          normflux=flux, subpix=5)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
And for multiple flux fractions:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
r, flag = sep.flux_radius(data, objs[\(aqx\(aq], objs[\(aqy\(aq], 6.*objs[\(aqa\(aq],
                          [0.5, 0.6], normflux=flux, subpix=5)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Equivalent of XWIN_IMAGE, YWIN_IMAGE in Source Extractor
.sp
Source Extractor\(aqs XWIN_IMAGE, YWIN_IMAGE parameters can be used for
more accurate object centroids than the default X_IMAGE, Y_IMAGE.
Here, the \fBwinpos\fP function provides this behavior.  To match Source
Extractor exactly, the right \fBsig\fP parameter (giving a description
of the effective width) must be used for each object.  Source
Extractor uses \fB2.  / 2.35 * (half\-light radius)\fP where the
half\-light radius is calculated using \fBflux_radius\fP with a fraction
of 0.5 and a normalizing flux of \fBFLUX_AUTO\fP\&. The equivalent here is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
sig = 2. / 2.35 * r  # r from sep.flux_radius() above, with fluxfrac = 0.5
xwin, ywin, flag = sep.winpos(data, objs[\(aqx\(aq], objs[\(aqy\(aq], sig)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Segmentation\-masked image measurements
.sp
SourceExtractor provides a mechanism for measuring the \(dqAUTO\(dq and
\(dqFLUX_RADIUS\(dq parameters for a given object including a mask for
neighboring sources. In addition to the mask, setting the SourceExtractor
parameter \fBMASK_TYPE=CORRECT\fP further fills the masked pixels of a given
source with \(dqgood\(dq pixel values reflected opposite of the masked pixels.
The \fBSEP\fP photometry and measurement functions provide an option for
simple masking without reflection or subtracting neighbor flux.
.sp
For example, using a segmentation array provided by \fBsep.extract\fP, we
can compute the masked \fBflux_radius\fP that could otherwise be
artificially large due to flux from nearby sources:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# list of object id numbers that correspond to the segments
seg_id = np.arange(1, len(objs)+1, dtype=np.int32)

r, flag = sep.flux_radius(data, objs[\(aqx\(aq], objs[\(aqy\(aq], 6.*objs[\(aqa\(aq],
                          0.5, seg_id=seg_id, seg=seg,
                          normflux=flux, subpix=5)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
To enforce that a given measurement \fBonly\fP includes pixels within a
segment, provide negative values in the \fBseg_id\fP list.  Otherwise the
mask for a given object will be pixels with
\fB(seg == 0) | (seg_id == id_i)\fP\&.
.sp
The following functions include the segmentation masking:
\fBsum_circle\fP, \fBsum_circann\fP, \fBsum_ellipse\fP, \fBsum_ellipann\fP,
\fBflux_radius\fP , and \fBkron_radius\fP (\fBwinpos\fP \fBcurrently does not\fP).
.SS Masking image regions
.sp
Create a boolean array with elliptical regions set to True:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
mask = np.zeros(data.shape, dtype=np.bool)
sep.mask_ellipse(mask, objs[\(aqx\(aq], objs[\(aqy\(aq], obs[\(aqa\(aq], objs[\(aqb\(aq],
                 objs[\(aqtheta\(aq], r=3.)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Changelog
.SS Upgrading From kbarbary/sep
.SS Changes to the C API
.sp
This page details cumulative changes to the C API between v1.2.1 and
v1.4.0, or since this package was forked from
kbarbary/sep \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:kbarbary/\:sep>\&. Almost all changes to
the public API are to integer parameters, many of which have been changed
from \fBint\fP to \fBint64_t\fP, to fix problems which may arise with
extremely large arrays. The other change of note is to the \fBsep_image\fP
struct, which enables the user to pass an existing segmentation map, and
re\-extract all morphological and photometric quantities on a different
image.
.sp
All changes here are transparent to users of the Python interface.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
